WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:08.200
SIERRA: Neighbors. Everyone has them, and
the kind you have can make a big difference

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.420
in your life. They can be frustrating or they
can be a godsend, it all depends. Countries

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:20.440
are in the same situation. Good neighbors
can mean beneficial security and trade agreements.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:26.039
Bad neighbors can mean border crises, conflict
over resources, and even war. Today we are

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:30.730
looking at one of our neighbors - Mexico.
By most measures they re a good neighbor - it

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.460
s been a very long time since there was any
military conflict between our two nations,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.809
and Mexico is arguably the United States most
important trade partner. This closeness has

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:45.579
allowed the U.S. to rely on peace in its own
neighborhood, and to focus on other issues

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:51.710
abroad. Still, a lot remains misunderstood
about Mexico in the United States, particularly

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:57.620
when it comes to hot button issues like trade,
immigration and drug trafficking. I'm Gabrielle

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.820
Sierra, and this is Why It Matters. Today,
Mexico.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:09.150
Montage: Mexico, Mexico, Mexico, Mexico, Mexico,
Mexico, Mexico, Mexico

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:20.670
SIERRA: So let's do a little Mexico 101. If
I know nothing about Mexico, what are some

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.710
things that I should know?

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:28.640
O'NEIL: So Mexico is our Southern neighbor.
We share a 2,000 mile border with the country.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.369
It's a country of about 120 million people.
It is a democracy though recently has been

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:37.320
struggling as such.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.480
SIERRA: This is Shannon O'Neil, she s a senior
fellow for Latin America studies at the Council

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.330
on Foreign Relations.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:49.369
O'NEIL: When you look around the world, there's
probably no country that affects more what

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.600
happens day to day in the United States than
Mexico. So the vegetables and fruits that

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:00.180
are on your table that you eat every morning
or every evening, the appliances that you

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.610
buy, the other goods that you purchase that
you fill your houses with, the communities

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:08.990
in which you live and the restaurants that
are down the street perhaps and the broader

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:14.901
culture, all of those things as well as today
some 11 million Mexicans and 35 million Mexican

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:20.349
Americans who are a big part of the US population.
So the communities that so many of us exist

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:26.110
in and around too, are also tied to Mexico.
It is our most important trading partner,

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:32.200
ahead of Canada and ahead of China. It is
important in terms of our shared environment

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:38.250
along the border, in terms of our industries
as well as our waterways. And it is important

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:45.330
in terms of security, in terms of border security,
but in terms of security overall and the joint

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.310
fight against organized crime.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:52.830
SIERRA: Each of the connections Shannon just
mentioned are at times headline issues in

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:58.849
the United States. But among them, the economic
relationship may be the most important, and

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:05.940
least understood. In 2019, more than $677
billion dollars in trade was conducted between

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:12.049
Mexico and the United States, affecting 5
million jobs. Again, that s higher than overall

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:17.580
trade with China in the same year. And underneath
the big numbers, the ties go deeper.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:24.390
O'NEIL: And so today interestingly, when you
look at products in the United States, Mexico

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:30.680
and Canada are integral to the making of those
products and these countries are big buyers

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:37.400
of US goods and services when they make products.
So for instance, a product that comes in from

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.650
Mexico, so it was imported into the United
States from Mexico, on average, about 40%

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.680
of that product was actually made in the United
States. So US workers put things together

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.500
that are then sent to Mexico to be assembled.
If you look at products coming in from China,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.870
it's less than 4% that was made in the United
States. So pretty much everything is made

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.659
on the other side of the ocean. So Mexico
and the United States are incredibly important

00:03:57.659 --> 00:03:59.990
to each other because they make things together.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:05.319
SIERRA: But this level of cooperation hasn
t always been the case. And it s not something

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:07.020
that should be taken for granted.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:14.810
O'NEIL: So Mexico, if you look back in the
1980s, it was really a country that sold commodities

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.070
north to the United States, primarily oil
and some agricultural products, and it was

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:24.690
a quite closed economy. So it had a lot of
tariffs and other kinds of barriers that kept

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:30.100
the US from selling goods to the south. The
big shift really happened in the early 1990s

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.660
with the formation and signing of NAFTA, the
North American Free Trade Agreement that brought

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.440
Mexico, the United States, and Canada together
in a free trade region. And you see in the

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:46.000
wake of NAFTA over the next decade, trade
grow exponentially between the countries,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.460
and over these last 25, 30 years, the nature
of that trade, not only has it grown fourfold,

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.400
but it has also changed. So it's gone from
commodities, from selling oil, from selling

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.500
fruits and vegetables and like which still
are sold, but to advanced manufacturing products.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:07.910
So auto parts and medical devices and airplanes
and all kinds of other types of equipment

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.100
and higher tech goods are now sold between
all of these countries.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:19.050
SIERRA: How did life in Mexico change for
people after the country embraced free trade?

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.720
And what has that meant for the United States?

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.690
CAMPERO: I mean, let me just give you an anecdote.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:30.110
SIERRA: This is Mariana Campero, she s an
expert in the Americas program at CSIS. She

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:36.870
is also the host of the Mexico Matters podcast.
And former CEO of the Mexican Council on Foreign

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.350
Relations, COMEXI.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:44.880
CAMPERO: When I was a little girl, I remember
my trips to the United States as one of the

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:51.400
most incredible things in the world, because
I was able to buy US candy. My mother bought

00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:02.060
US toothpaste and shampoos. It sounds trivial,
but in Mexico, we did not have, in our shelves,

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:08.340
US products, or very little of them. And those
that existed in Mexico were very expensive.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:15.590
So one of the things that NAFTA, when it came
into effect, it radically changed the life

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:22.759
of Mexican consumers, because suddenly we
were able to have every product from every

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:29.669
part of the world, and with the best quality
at cheaper prices. Sort of more importantly,

00:06:29.669 --> 00:06:36.699
because of NAFTA, Mexico, especially the Northern
part of Mexico which was closer to the United

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:42.569
States, and so therefore transport costs were
lower, was able to develop, and it became

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:49.940
a true manufacturing powerhouse. The Mexican
economy was able to grow its Northern parts

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:57.009
at rates that were close to Asian countries.
Some parts of the Northern grew at 10%. That

00:06:57.009 --> 00:07:05.000
also allowed for a lot of the people in the
south to move north to work in these maquiladoras

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.430
or manufacturing facilities.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:15.539
SIERRA: NAFTA eliminated taxes on imported
and exported goods, as long as they met certain

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:20.720
manufacturing requirements, allowing products,
parts, and services to be traded essentially

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.509
for free. As a result, prices went down, and
choices became more abundant. This benefited

00:07:26.509 --> 00:07:31.570
businesses, shareholders, and consumers, and
many economists would say that on net NAFTA

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:36.630
was a good thing. But not everyone shares
this opinion. NAFTA was controversial from

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:42.020
the moment it was signed, and over the years,
many politicians, and many workers, have blamed

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.729
NAFTA for US job losses.

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:49.060
Bill CLINTON: In a few moments I will sign
the North American Free Trade Act into law,

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.260
NAFTA will tear down trade barriers between
our three nations.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:57.449
Ross PEROT: When you ve not a 7:1 wage differential
between the U.S. and Mexico you will hear

00:07:57.449 --> 00:07:58.500
the giant sucking sound.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.810
Donald TRUMP: NAFTA is the worst trade deal
maybe ever signed anywhere, but certainly

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.120
ever signed in this country.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.199
Bernie SANDERS: Corporate America and the
big money interests have told us that NAFTA

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:13.949
is a good deal, I don t believe them.

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:18.960
SIERRA: So in some political circles, I feel
like NAFTA was also seen as something that

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.060
cost American jobs. Is that the case?

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:27.159
O'NEIL: Well, it's interesting. When you look
at the decade after NAFTA was signed, sort

00:08:27.159 --> 00:08:32.280
of the 1990s, jobs in all three countries
increased and the sales between all three

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.940
countries increased. And in fact, Mexico was
one of the biggest consumer markets for US

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:43.380
goods. Around 2001, that starts to change.
And one of the big changes is that China comes

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:49.279
into the WTO and you see more and more goods
coming from China to the rest of the world.

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:53.060
So that, you know, we talk about here in the
United States, the China shock and the effect

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:58.150
that China had on US goods and sales and US
jobs. Well, they'd had a similar effect and

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:03.760
perhaps even a bigger effect on jobs in Mexico.
So whole industries that were based in Mexico,

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:09.779
textiles and toy making and shoe making, so
much of that disappeared. And as it disappeared,

00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:15.810
so did US-based suppliers that used to sell
the leather for shoes or the high heels or

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.070
the sorts of things down there. So you see
this symbiotic relationship. So that first

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:24.720
decade when China really wasn't part of the
picture, you saw jobs increase throughout

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.180
the three countries. Over the 2000s and into
the 2010s, you saw jobs fall in all three

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:35.829
countries, but in part because of challenges
and competition from other parts of the world.

00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:38.690
SIERRA: So is NAFTA still in place?

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:44.740
O'NEIL: So NAFTA was in place until just about
a year plus ago when it was replaced with

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:51.310
the USMCA, the US, Mexico, and Canada Agreement.
So this is basically a NAFTA update. It kept

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:55.670
some of the things in the initial agreement.
It added things that hadn't really been thought

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:01.810
about in 1992, in 1993 when the initial agreement
was negotiated. So things like the internet

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:06.540
and e-commerce, digital trade and the like,
digital information and how you store it,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:12.500
or the privacy rights. So all of those are
new, bigger aspects of the USMCA that weren't

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.920
all there in the original NAFTA.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.279
SIERRA: All right, so I have to train myself
to use a new acronym.

00:10:17.279 --> 00:10:19.160
O'NEIL: I know. All the time.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.220
SIERRA: All the time.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:27.470
SIERRA: There s a reason that NAFTA, and the
USMCA, have gotten so much attention in the

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:33.490
news and in election cycles. Jobs mean a lot
to voters. And because feelings about them

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:38.529
run so deep, politicians on both sides of
the aisle have often taken up these trade

00:10:38.529 --> 00:10:44.110
agreements as campaign issues. It s a clear
example of how our relationship with Mexico

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:49.000
shows up at the center of national debates.
There s another big issue that shows up in

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:57.889
the same way, one that is often just as misunderstood.
Immigration. This year, the number of undocumented

00:10:57.889 --> 00:11:04.750
migrants at the southern border hit the highest
level in over 20 years. Migrants came from

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:09.269
around the world as Covid and its economic
consequences drove ever higher numbers of

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:10.620
people from their homes.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:17.639
SIERRA: Let's turn to the issue that we hear
about most often, especially over these past

00:11:17.639 --> 00:11:24.529
politically tense years here in the US, immigration.
So what have some of the most important inflection

00:11:24.529 --> 00:11:28.410
points been as this issue has played out over
the years?

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:34.570
O'NEIL: So for many years, Mexico was the
biggest source of migrants to the United States.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:40.579
And you saw an uptick start in 1970s and 1980s,
continue in the 1990s and hit a real high

00:11:40.579 --> 00:11:45.560
in the early 2000s where Mexicans were coming
to the United States for a whole host of reasons.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:50.910
There had been economic booms and busts that
drove people north. There was a demographic

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:55.880
boom. So lots of Mexicans were turning 16,
17, 18 every year and didn't really have anywhere

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:00.470
to go in terms of their own labor market.
And you also saw throughout the 1990s, Mexico

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:04.769
struggling to democratize, to move from an
authoritarian system to a democratic system

00:12:04.769 --> 00:12:09.269
and many people and families were pushed out
by the authoritarian government before that.

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:13.590
So there's lots of reasons that were pushing
Mexicans to come to the United States. You

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:19.250
have seen over the last 10 to almost 15 years
a decline in Mexican migration to the United

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:23.720
States. And here again, there are lots of
reasons. So part of it is the economy has

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:26.750
been doing better there. You haven't seen
the wild swings in the past that would lead

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:31.459
people to search for opportunities in different
places. You've seen a more stable political

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:36.110
system and a democracy. So many people feel
their voices are heard now when they had not

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:43.090
been in the past. And crucially, you also
see changing numbers of people who turn 15,

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:48.940
16, 17 every year and are trying to enter
into the labor market. So compared to 2000,

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:54.130
they're anywhere between 100,000 and 200,000
fewer people turning 18 every year that are

00:12:54.130 --> 00:13:00.410
even looking for a job. So there's more space
in the labor market in Mexico than in the

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:01.649
past.

00:13:01.649 --> 00:13:08.350
CAMPERO: In the last few years, you know,
just before the pandemic and before this administration,

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:12.950
Mexican migration to the U.S. was almost net
zero. That means that the flows of people

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:18.880
coming into the United States were more or
less equal to the people coming back to Mexico,

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:23.850
because there were job opportunities that
were created in Mexico on a yearly basis.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:30.070
SIERRA: That s right Mexico is close to net
zero in terms of immigration to the United

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:34.510
States. Though that fact is often lost in
media coverage and political debate.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:43.149
Al Jazeera: The U.N. has urged the Trump administration
to immediately stop separating migrant children

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:46.170
from their families at its border with Mexico.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:50.750
Donald TRUMP: When Mexico sends its people
they re not sending their best.

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:58.800
ABC News: More than 172,00 migrants were stopped
at the border last month, the most in 20 years.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.240
O'NEIL: So the migration today that we're
talking a lot about in the United States and

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:08.070
have been for the last number of years is
not about Mexican migration as much, but it's

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:14.710
about migration from other places that comes
through Mexico. So since 2014, 2015, we have

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:20.310
seen waves of Central Americans coming up
through Mexico to come to the United States.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:25.089
And we've obviously seen it just this last
year in 2021, increases from Central America

00:14:25.089 --> 00:14:31.391
driven by violence and poverty and bad governance
and corruption and all sorts of factors. Many

00:14:31.391 --> 00:14:35.139
Central Americans are leaving and looking
for life elsewhere and many pulled to the

00:14:35.139 --> 00:14:36.139
United States.

00:14:36.139 --> 00:14:43.079
CAMPERO: The biggest spike that we saw in
the Northern border was migrants from Central

00:14:43.079 --> 00:14:50.850
America, particularly from the Northern triangle.
What we're seeing recently is that Mexico

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:59.500
is not only an export country, it became a
transit country, and now it is becoming a

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:07.040
receiving country. So now Mexico also has
its own problem to deal with when the fact

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:12.269
is that a lot of the people that were not
able to stay in the United States or to cross

00:15:12.269 --> 00:15:19.339
the border into the United States, want to
stay in Mexico. And they have asked for asylum

00:15:19.339 --> 00:15:29.850
in Mexico, and this has put tremendous pressure
into our own communities, into our own governmental

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:38.220
authorities that are trying to cope with thousands
and thousands of people. So Mexico has a huge

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:45.089
problem to deal with and we don't have not
even close to the resources, nor the capabilities,

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:52.089
to really deal with this issue in a humane
or in a professional manner.

00:15:52.089 --> 00:15:57.490
O'NEIL: So recently we've seen lots of Haitians,
tens of thousands of Haitians coming, but

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:00.990
we see people coming through Mexico from all
different parts of the world, sometimes as

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:06.130
far as Asia or as Africa, but coming to Mexico
and then coming up to the US border seeking

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:10.710
asylum or seeking to come in through other
or means to try to find their way in the United

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:16.290
States. So Mexico has become a gateway for
migration to the United States. And so there's

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:20.740
two things here. One is that is a tension,
as we've seen in the United States both in

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:24.630
the US trying to regulate the migration that
comes into the United States. The other challenge

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:30.120
is Mexico is absorbing tens of thousands,
indeed more than that, of these migrants into

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:34.440
Mexico. So they get, especially Central Americans
get part way into Mexico and then they end

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:40.149
up staying there as do Haitians as do others.
So this is a nation that is a middle income

00:16:40.149 --> 00:16:44.430
country, has its own challenges with poverty
and job creation and the like and they are

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:47.839
absorbing now many migrants as well.

00:16:47.839 --> 00:16:52.639
SIERRA: So then are the US and Mexico working
together on immigration?

00:16:52.639 --> 00:16:58.790
O'NEIL: The US and Mexico have been working
together on immigration largely over the last

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:05.419
few years to close or limit the movement of
people into the United States across the US-Mexico

00:17:05.419 --> 00:17:10.579
border. We have seen over the last nine or
10 months under the Biden administration,

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:15.380
some changes in those rules and the setting
up and expanding of the immigration system

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:20.300
at the border to process people in quicker
and more comprehensive ways. So you're starting

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:25.390
to see more families particularly and mothers
and others with kids come through and be processed

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:29.799
and brought into the United States. So there
is cooperation on managing the system at the

00:17:29.799 --> 00:17:35.179
border. But I will say this has been one of
the most difficult parts of the relationship

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:40.789
because it is hard to do, to know what to
do, and to build capacity to manage tens of

00:17:40.789 --> 00:17:45.080
thousands, hundreds of thousands of people
that are flowing through. And one of the bigger

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:50.620
challenges too is that Mexico just doesn't
have the capacity to do that.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:57.169
SIERRA: How we build this capacity is an ongoing
political debate. Many ideas have been floated,

00:17:57.169 --> 00:18:03.600
like increasing funds for child welfare professionals,
translators, and medical staff at border facilities,

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:08.591
and initiating community housing programs
to limit the use of detention centers. Many

00:18:08.591 --> 00:18:13.400
of these ideas might be effective, but big
change is a hard sell on both sides of the

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:19.799
border. One important step is recognizing
that it s a shared problem. The migrants seeking

00:18:19.799 --> 00:18:24.880
asylum have escaped prosecution and violence
in their home countries, and in order to get

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:30.549
to the U.S., they need to cross through Mexico.
As a result, Mexico has had to shoulder significant

00:18:30.549 --> 00:18:35.941
burdens of its own due to the migrant crisis,
and is scrambling to find a solution, just

00:18:35.941 --> 00:18:42.120
as the U.S. is. Misunderstanding about shared
problems emerges as a theme over and over

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:47.140
again in US-Mexico relations. And that is
certainly the case when it comes to another

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:52.140
issue that has persisted for decades: drug-trafficking
and gang violence.

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:57.770
Al Jazeera: More than 300,000 people have
been killed in Mexico since the start of the

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:04.090
US-led war on drugs in 2006. And today, Mexico
continues to make headlines as one of the

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.200
most dangerous countries in Latin America.

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:10.250
CBS Mornings: Officials along the southern
border say Mexican drug cartels are moving

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:15.820
much larger shipments of drugs into the US.
The amount of fentanyl, an especially dangerous

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:21.630
substance, that was confiscated by the Texas
Department of Public Safety jumped 800% in

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:24.630
just one year.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:31.400
O'NEIL: Security is a huge issue in the US-Mexico
relations and it goes both ways. Mexico has

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:36.450
since the late '80s through the '90s become
one of the biggest sources of drugs that are

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:41.770
imported into the United States, starting
with marijuana and cocaine, and then moving

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:46.490
into 2010s to meth, and then today, much of
the fentanyl that comes into the United States,

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:51.679
it's from abroad comes through Mexico. The
precursors come from China mostly, but they

00:19:51.679 --> 00:19:57.750
come through Mexico. So it is a huge source
of illegal drugs that come into the United

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:02.000
States. On the other side, the United States
ships back lots of money that pays for those

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:06.350
drugs, as well as guns that go to Mexico that
many of these cartels and organized crime

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:11.440
buy. So there's a illegal trade happening
in both directions, both of them problematic

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:16.039
and harmful to the two societies on either
side. And what we see, obviously we have huge

00:20:16.039 --> 00:20:20.990
costs here in the United States due to illegal
drugs and particularly in recent years, the

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:26.049
rise of fentanyl. We saw 70,000 Americans
die last year from fentanyl and opioid overdoses.

00:20:26.049 --> 00:20:32.200
You know, in Mexico, we saw a record high
homicides last year as well and largely tied

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:38.840
to organized crime and just the violence that's
associated with illegal activities, drug trafficking

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:44.010
being one of them, contraband being others,
human trafficking and migrants, as well as

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:49.340
other types of illegal activities, especially
extortion and others on the ground. So insecurity

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:54.350
on both sides of the border feed into each
other and make both countries less safe.

00:20:54.350 --> 00:21:01.169
SIERRA: It s a tragic feedback loop, and the
United States contributes to it in complicated

00:21:01.169 --> 00:21:06.600
ways. For example, it s estimated that half
a million guns are smuggled from the U.S.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:12.370
to Mexico each year. The problem is so severe
that the Mexican government has filed a lawsuit

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:17.670
against several US gun companies, alleging
that they knowingly supply the criminal gun

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:18.670
market.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:27.240
CAMPERO: The fact that the United States has
not been able or to curb the sale of arms

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:33.789
to the Mexican cartels has become an irritant.
From a Mexican point of view, I don't think

00:21:33.789 --> 00:21:42.090
we have fully recognized our own fault and
our lack of rule of law that obviously has

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:49.220
allowed the Mexican cartels to grow and be
all-powerful, and they are even a threat to

00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:50.400
the state.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:59.159
SIERRA: In 2020, there were 34,515 homicides
in Mexico, a per capita rate several times

00:21:59.159 --> 00:22:05.160
higher than in the United States. In the most
recent midterm election 89 politicians, including

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:11.270
35 candidates, were killed. And that s not
all, according to Mexico s National Search

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:17.441
Commission, there are 100,000 unresolved cases
of missing people in the country, a problem

00:22:17.441 --> 00:22:19.919
that goes back decades.

00:22:19.919 --> 00:22:27.340
SIERRA: It's difficult for me to imagine a
reality in which organized crime has so much

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:32.429
power, a world where it might be a relatively
common experience to have a friend or a loved

00:22:32.429 --> 00:22:39.840
one disappear and to suspect or know that
it likely involved a cartel.

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:45.350
CAMPERO: Yeah, I agree with you. I think it's
a horrible reality, and in Mexico, it is a

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:51.690
reality that a lot of people have lived. I
mean, you probably remember the case a few

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:59.130
years ago in which a students just disappeared
and they they have not been found. So their

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:04.980
bodies were burned or what happened to their
bodies, it's really unclear yet. There has

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:12.970
not been a full investigation as to what happened
to those students. But it is the reality of

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:20.610
a lot of mothers in Mexico have taken upon
themselves to go out and find their own loved

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:27.490
ones, and they have found mass graves, in
which they have sort of done the work to identify

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:34.070
what are the remains of their own family members.
It is a reality, unfortunately, for a lot

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:37.140
of people in a lot of Mexican communities.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:42.990
SIERRA: Do we see the US and Mexico cooperating
on security problems?

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:50.630
O'NEIL: The US and Mexico have had moments
of close cooperation on this. And in fact,

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:54.460
in 2007, 2008, we saw a whole new initiative
called the M rida Initiative which was signed

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:59.130
between at the time President Calderone in
Mexico and Bush here in the United States

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:04.150
that really opened up security cooperation
in ways that hadn't been there for many years.

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:08.760
That has in more recent years fallen somewhat
by the wayside. And in fact, the current president,

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:12.270
L pez Obrador, and his foreign minister have
said that it's not working and they want to

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:16.900
renegotiate it all. And you see frustration
in the US Capitol on the Congress as well

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:23.190
as the executive branch in security cooperation
attenuating or sort of dissipating in recent

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:29.230
years. So I think this is a real challenge
for the two countries because it is so tied.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:34.190
You can't fix the problem in one place without
also addressing it in the other. So the security

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:39.130
cooperation, there have been strong moments
in the bilateral relationship and in the history,

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.470
but today isn't one of them.

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:47.330
SIERRA: Trade, migration, drugs and security.
These are complex challenges that hit deep

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:52.529
political nerves in both Mexico and the United
States. And their persistence through the

00:24:52.529 --> 00:24:58.200
years raises questions about how leaders on
both sides of the wall are handling the relationship.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:03.690
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit about
Mexico's current president known to the world

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:04.690
as AMLO?

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:09.320
O'NEIL: Yes. So his full name is Andr s Manuel
L pez Obrador, but everybody calls him AMLO

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:13.900
because you couldn't say that every day. He
has been running for president or he had been

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:16.990
running for president for many, many years.
He lost two elections. Then he finally won

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:25.500
on the third time, which was in 2018. He is
seen as outside of the normal political elites.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:29.130
He talks with a bit of a different accent.
He speaks much more slowly than people from

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:35.530
Mexico City, and he positions himself as being
outside of those circles of fancy people or

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:40.450
what he calls them fifis. So sort of those
who are elite and have traveled and speak

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:46.270
English and do other kinds of things. He sees
that as not his base. That he is tied to the

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:50.460
more average Mexican and the issues that they
deal with. So he paints himself really as

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:56.350
a populist, as an outsider, and came in with
a really sweeping majority So that was the

00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:59.620
wave that brought him into power a few years
ago.

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:02.100
SIERRA: Seems like there's a but coming here.

00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:07.470
O'NEIL: There is a but coming. And so while
he came in and he said he was going to tackle

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:11.670
crime and he was going to tackle corruption,
which were the two things Mexicans most wanted

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:17.380
change on, he has not. And in fact, he if
anything is going in the opposite direction.

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:24.029
So under his watch, we have seen homicides
and crimes increase, not decrease as he has

00:26:24.029 --> 00:26:30.440
shaken up the overall security situation not
funded or underfunded state level and local

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:34.780
level police officers in favor of the military
and things like that. So he hasn't fixed the

00:26:34.780 --> 00:26:39.559
security problem or started to diminish the
violence and the real challenges that people

00:26:39.559 --> 00:26:41.440
face day to day in their life.

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:46.910
O'NEIL: And he also came in riding this anti-corruption
crusade that he would stop the backroom deals

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:51.039
and the stealing that unfortunately did happen
in the Mexican government and throughout very

00:26:51.039 --> 00:26:56.240
parts of society and the like, and unfortunately
that has not happened either. And there have

00:26:56.240 --> 00:27:03.480
been some videos of his family members taking
bribes in various cases in cafeterias and

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:07.880
the like and wondering where that money goes.
So there's a feeling there that things really

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:09.570
haven't changed.

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:15.780
SIERRA: What is the Biden-AMLO relationship
like? Do they get along?

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:21.630
O'NEIL: Well, interestingly, Trump and AMLO
got along very well. I think for two reasons.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:26.600
One is they have somewhat similar temperaments.
They are believe very strongly, very self-assured

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:31.039
in their own ability to do things, they see
themselves as messiahs or leaders in many

00:27:31.039 --> 00:27:35.669
ways, but the other real reason is that Trump
didn't ask very much of AMLO. All he asked

00:27:35.669 --> 00:27:40.529
is that he stopped migration to the border.
But all the other things AMLO was doing in

00:27:40.529 --> 00:27:48.130
Mexico, the various economic changes or political
changes or AMLO's attacking of the press and

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:51.510
these things, Trump didn't care very much
about at all. In fact, didn't care about.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:55.470
Even security issues, he really didn't press.
And so I think it was an easy relationship

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:56.470
for AMLO.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:00.200
O'NEIL: So fast forward to Biden, the Biden
administration is much broader. You see them,

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:03.679
yes, of course they care about migration and
we see that as continuing, but they care about

00:28:03.679 --> 00:28:08.070
security issues, they care about economic
issues, they care about labor issues, they

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:12.870
care about democratic checks and balances,
human rights, these sorts of things and that

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:17.669
interest of the United States and what's happening
inside Mexico in the domestic agenda I think

00:28:17.669 --> 00:28:20.720
is quite uncomfortable for AMLO and he's let
that be known.

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:27.649
O'NEIL: So I think there is a real challenge
in Mexico today as he has a very strong agenda

00:28:27.649 --> 00:28:33.481
where he wants to roll back energy reforms
and bring the energy sector back to the Mexican

00:28:33.481 --> 00:28:37.740
government, the control of the Mexican government.
He wants to kick out foreign investors and

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:43.090
others that have been there, particularly
in this sector. He wants to limit the role

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:48.450
of political opposition and political opponents
and the like. So there has been already a

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:51.410
democratic backsliding within the country.

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:57.029
SIERRA: It s unclear where that democratic
backsliding is headed, but like so much else

00:28:57.029 --> 00:29:02.560
with Mexico, it s worth keeping an eye on.
Because walls aside, what happens in Mexico

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:06.309
will continue to affect life in the United
States, every day.

00:29:06.309 --> 00:29:11.269
O'NEIL: One of the most important reasons
to root for a stable Mexico is that they are

00:29:11.269 --> 00:29:16.799
at our border. So if Mexico does well, the
United States will do well. If Mexico is doing

00:29:16.799 --> 00:29:22.250
poorly, then it will be a huge problem for
the United States. And Mexico through the

00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:27.500
last several decades has been with all of
its ups and downs, a pretty stable and safe

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:32.840
and good neighbor to the United States. We
have a neighbor that does not have terrorism,

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:37.690
we have a neighbor that has a quite stable
economy, we have a neighbor that, yes, has

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:42.570
sent us migrants, but those have enriched
very much our economy and many of them have

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:48.059
then headed back and gone back to Mexico.
And we have a neighbor who for the last 25

00:29:48.059 --> 00:29:49.830
plus years has been a democracy.

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:55.850
O'NEIL: And I think all of those things, stability,
the shared values, and the economic ties that

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:59.570
have developed between the two countries have
made the United States safer, have made the

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:03.350
border more stable, and have actually made
the United States more competitive. So the

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:08.240
flip side of that is if you did not have a
democratic Mexico, if you did not have a stable

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:12.659
Mexico, if you saw organized crime and others
grow to the point where they controlled the

00:30:12.659 --> 00:30:17.700
territory, those would pose huge problems
for the United States, both the cities along

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:22.010
the border and the states along the border,
but for the overall economy given how linked

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:23.049
we are economically, societally, culturally,
and the like.

00:30:23.049 --> 00:30:24.750
SIERRA: This relationship has a direct impact
on the lives of millions of Americans. From

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:25.750
educational and cultural ties to our diplomatic
and economic endeavours, we are in it together.

00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:28.550
Within all of our shared responsibilities
is a long history of partnership. And for

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:35.300
all our problems, the basic relationship is
a good one, and there would be an enormous

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:41.641
amount to lose if the relationship deteriorates,
and it s something we shouldn t take for granted.

